# Per-account topic access, checked against the accounts in passwd (built by
# init-passwd.sh from the *_MQTT_PASS variables in .env). Nothing in this
# file is a secret -- usernames and topic shapes only.

# Applies to EVERY client, including anonymous ones (mosquitto.conf sets
# allow_anonymous true) -- rules with no preceding `user` line are global.
# Read-only, and deliberately covers everything under energy/# with no
# exceptions -- including energy/+/set, so anyone on the LAN can watch
# inverter commands go by with no account at all. That's intentional, not
# an oversight: reads never need a password here, only writes do (below).
topic read energy/#

# poller (app/poller.py): publishes its own raw state, command results, and
# derived state (PV correction/energy integration/bank aggregation --
# app/derive.py -- and the solar forecast, app/forecast.py, all computed
# in-process now rather than by a separate Node-RED flow). Listens for
# commands. No explicit read grant on energy/+/set here -- the global rule
# above already covers it.
user poller
topic write energy/+/state
topic write energy/+/set/result
topic write energy/derived/#

# settings: used only by solar_settings.html, to publish inverter commands.
# solar_dash.html and config.html are pure-read and connect with no MQTT
# account at all now -- the global anonymous rule above already covers
# them. This account exists purely so a stray or scripted publish from
# anywhere else on the LAN (or the anonymous path) can't accidentally
# change a setting; its password (SETTINGS_MQTT_PASS) is intentionally not
# a strong secret -- this ACL is what actually protects the inverter, not
# obscurity. Read access is already covered by the global rule above, so
# there's nothing to grant here beyond the write.
user settings
topic write energy/+/set
